 "shelfmark": "Rigsarkivet, TKUA, Speciel del, England: Gesandtskabsrelationer A III 52 (1653-66), Simon de Petkum",
 "seen": "Every image 337-657 was downloaded at 1000 px and viewed nine to a sheet (533 px a frame). Every page on which cipher showed was then viewed again as a half-frame at 500 px a page, where an interlinear gloss can be told from bare cipher. Images 338, 340, 359, 360 also as full-size crops.",
 "monsieur": {
  "identified_as": "Conrad Biermann (later ennobled von Ehrenschild), secretary of the German Chancery in Copenhagen",
  "evidence": "Address panels filmed with the letters: image 360 left, \"A Monsieur / Monsieur Bierman / Secretaire des affaires estrangeres de Sa Ma.te de Dennemarck Norvege &c / Coppenhaguen\"; image 616 left, \"A Monsieur / Mons. Bierman premier Secretaire de la Chambre de Sa Ma.te de Dennemarck Norvege &c. / a Coppenhagen\". The relations to the king cite \"la lettre du Secretaire Bierman\" as the channel for the king's orders (images 337 right, 342 right, 409 left, 421 right, 429 right, 461 right, 501 left).",
  "note": "The surname and offices are read from the leaves. The first name and the later title are from general knowledge of the Danish chancery, not from the unit; the project already has a Biermann target (targets/biermann1675)."
 },
 "correction_to_entry_2530": "The split is not \"king deciphered, Monsieur not\". The chancery also glossed most of the letters to Biermann between the lines (list in deciphered_leaves). The undeciphered leaves are the letters to Biermann of Jan, Mar-Jun 1665 in part, Sep 1665, and nearly everything from 23 Nov 1665 to Apr 1666, plus one relation to the king (6 Apr 1666). Image 477 right is headed \"Monsieur\", not \"Sire\".",
